import pf.Html
import presence.AvatarStack
import presence.LiveCursors
import presence.Stack
import presence.TypingIndicator
import ../../../Demos/PresenceDemo
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Demo
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The presence package: a room kept in the browser first (this site has no server), then how an
## app uses it, each component, how a room works, the stores, the modules, and what waits on the
## platform.
meta = {
	title: "Presence",
	description: "Who is here, who is typing and where they point, for Roc Solid apps: an avatar stack, a typing indicator and live cursors, over a room the server keeps.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "room", label: "A room, in your browser" },
		{ id: "use", label: "Using it" },
		{ id: "stack", label: "<AvatarStack>" },
		{ id: "typing", label: "<TypingIndicator>" },
		{ id: "cursors", label: "<LiveCursors>" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How a room works" },
		{ id: "stores", label: "The stores" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "styling", label: "Styling" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Status" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/presence</code>]}
				title="Who is here, who is typing, and where they point"
			>
				An avatar stack, a typing line and live cursors for a shared page, fed by a room the server keeps.
				Tabs come and go cleanly: a reload doesn't flicker, and a laptop that shuts is swept out.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Use this package when people look at the same page together, such as a board, a document or a quiz,
				and each should see who else is there. A <em>room</em> is the set of people on that page right now,
				each with a name, a colour and a status. The package gives you three components that show a room
				(<code>{"<AvatarStack>"}</code>, <code>{"<TypingIndicator>"}</code> and <code>{"<LiveCursors>"}</code>),
				the browser's side that joins one (<code>Presence.join!</code>), and the server's side that keeps it
				(<code>PresenceServer</code>), all on a route's <a href={"${Paths.platform_live}#channels"}>channel</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				A member with two tabs open is one face, and leaves when their last tab does. A page that moves to
				another room leaves at once. A tab that closes leaves a few seconds later, so a reload doesn't make it
				flicker out and in. A tab that vanishes without a word (a laptop shut, a network gone) is swept out once
				its heartbeats stop.
			</p>

			<Heading id="room">A room, in your browser</Heading>
			<p>
				This site is static files, with no server to keep a room, so the demo plays the server's part. It keeps
				a list of who is here and tells the package's stores and components what a room's channel would. Press a
				button, or let Auto (on from the start) do something every 1.5 seconds.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo>
			<div data-demo="presence"><PresenceDemo /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<p>
				A face that arrives grows in, and one that leaves fades out. A typing line runs out on its own, with no
				message to say the person stopped. A cursor glides to each place it is sent, fades once it has been still
				for a while (press Auto to stop them), and is forgotten after longer. The demo uses short times so you
				see each of these: four seconds for typing, three to fade and twenty to forget. A room joined with
				<code>Presence.join!</code> uses seven, five and thirty.
			</p>

			<Heading id="use">Using it</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in the app's header, as for any <a href={Paths.ecosystem}>package</a>. It is not a
				plugin, so there is nothing to add to the program:
			</p>
			<Code source={"app [program] { pf: platform \"…/server/platform/main.roc\", presence: \"…/packages/presence/main.roc\" }"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> copies the components and the browser's stores into the app, so both halves
				compile them. Import the components where you need them. Each takes its data as an accessor (a function
				ending in <code>!</code> that reads a value), so it shows whatever feeds it: a room's channel in an app,
				or, on this page, the demo.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.presence_use.file}, in part"} source={Sources.presence_use.rocx} />
			<p>
				In an app, a room is a route's <a href={"${Paths.platform_live}#channels"}>channel</a>, and its URL
				names the room. The channel's check lets a member in as the person the middleware says they are: their
				key and metadata come from the server, never from the page. Its hooks are
				<code>PresenceServer</code>'s, so a tab joins as it subscribes and leaves when its subscription ends.
				Here is the whole route from examples/board:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.presence_route.file} source={Sources.presence_route.rocx} />
			<p>
				The page joins the room with <code>Presence.join!</code>, given the channel. It subscribes, tells the
				room every 15 seconds that this tab is still here, and returns the accessors the components read
				(<code>members!</code>, <code>others!</code>, <code>typing!</code>, <code>cursors!</code>) and the functions
				that tell the room about this tab (<code>status!</code>, <code>update!</code>, <code>typed!</code>,
				<code>moved!</code>…). Everything the page
				says goes over the tab's one live stream, alongside its other channels; presence needs no endpoint of
				its own.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.presence_join.file}, in part"} source={Sources.presence_join.rocx} />
			<p>
				A status is one of four: <code>Online</code> (the start), <code>Away</code>, <code>Busy</code> and
				<code>Offline</code>, so a typo is a compile error and the avatar stack knows how to show each. Set it
				from a handler or an effect with <code>{"(here.status!)(Busy)"}</code>. To show a hidden tab as away,
				add one line: <code>{"<Document on:visibilitychange.visible={here.visible!} />"}</code>. While the tab is
				hidden it is <code>Away</code> and its cursor is gone; when it shows again, its status comes back.
			</p>
			<p>
				Who a member is, <code>Wire.Meta(d)</code>, is what the components show (a name, a picture, a colour, a
				status) plus <code>data</code>, the app's own, of any type JSON can carry and that can be compared with
				<code>==</code> (<code>{"{}"}</code> for none). The channel's check gives the first; the page changes it
				with <code>{"(here.update!)(data)"}</code>, up to a kibibyte of JSON. On the board, it is the card a
				person has open (<code>{"{ card : Str }"}</code>), which each tile shows. The name, picture and colour
				stay the server's. A room whose <code>data</code> only the server may set refuses <code>Update</code>
				in its own <code>receive!</code>. <code>{"Wire.meta(name, data)"}</code> makes a member's metadata with
				the rest left to the components.
			</p>

			<Heading id="stack">{"<AvatarStack>"}</Heading>
			<div class="sample">
				<AvatarStack faces!={faces} max={4} />
			</div>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>{"faces! : () => List(Stack.Face)"}</code></td>
							<td>
								who is here, in order: <code>{"{ key, name, image, color, status }"}</code>.
								<code>Wire.face</code> makes one from a member. A face with an empty <code>image</code> shows
								its initials on its <code>color</code>; with an empty <code>color</code> too, it gets a colour
								picked from its key, the same one every time
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"max : U64 ?? 5"}</code></td><td>how many places the stack takes, counting the <code>+N</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"label : Str ?? \"\""}</code></td><td>what a screen reader hears for the stack; when empty, it says who is here: <code>"Ann, Bo and 2 others are here"</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Each face gets its status as a class, such as <code>status-away</code>. <code>Online</code> shows as it
				is: the stack is who is here, so being here needs no mark. <code>Away</code> dims the face and adds an
				orange dot, <code>Busy</code> adds a red dot, and <code>Offline</code> greys the face (examples/quiz
				shows a player who dropped so). A screen reader hears the status after the name: "Alan Turing (away)".
				The <code>+N</code>'s tooltip names the people it stands for.
			</p>

			<Heading id="typing">{"<TypingIndicator>"}</Heading>
			<div class="sample">
				<TypingIndicator names!={["Ada Lovelace", "Alan Turing"]} />
			</div>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>{"names! : () => List(Str)"}</code></td><td>who is typing; <code>Wire.names(members, keys)</code> turns the room's keys into names</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"say : (List(Str) -> Str) ?? Typing.sentence"}</code></td><td>writes the sentence: <code>"Ann is typing…"</code>, <code>"Ann and Bo are typing…"</code>, <code>"Ann, Bo and 2 others are typing…"</code>. Give your own for another language</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				It shows three bobbing dots and the sentence. When no one is typing, the line keeps its height, so
				nothing under it jumps. A screen reader hears it change (<code>aria-live="polite"</code>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="cursors">{"<LiveCursors>"}</Heading>
		</NoHydration>
		<div class="sample">
			<LiveCursors cursors!={cursors}>
				<div class="area"><p>What everyone points at</p></div>
			</LiveCursors>
		</div>
		<NoHydration>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>{"cursors! : () => List(LiveCursors.Pointer)"}</code></td>
							<td>
								others' cursors, <code>{"{ key, name, color, x, y, idle }"}</code>. <code>x</code> and
								<code>y</code> are fractions of the area, from 0 to 1, so a place is the same on a narrow
								screen and a wide one. <code>Wire.pointers(members, cursors)</code> makes them
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"children : List(Html)"}</code></td><td>the area they point at</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"on_move! : (Html.Point => {}) ?? |_| {}"}</code></td><td>called with your own pointer's place over the area (fractions of it), at most once a frame. Pass a room's <code>here.moved!</code>, which sends it at most 20 times a second</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"on_leave! : (() => {}) ?? || {}"}</code></td><td>called when your pointer leaves the area. Pass a room's <code>here.away!</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Each cursor is an arrow in its member's colour, with their name beside it. It glides to each place it
				is sent and fades when idle. Cursors take no pointer events, so clicks reach what is under them. To
				send your own, write <code>{"<LiveCursors … on_move!={here.moved!} on_leave!={here.away!}>"}</code>.
				Move your pointer over the demo's note: it shows as the others would see it, labelled You. Because it
				listens to the pointer, <code>{"<LiveCursors>"}</code> must be outside a
				<code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code>, as the sample above is.
			</p>

			<Heading id="how">How a room works</Heading>
			<p>
				<strong>The server keeps the room.</strong> It keeps each room in its own memory, so there is no broker
				to run. <code>Room</code> is the room's state: who is here, with their metadata, since when, and from
				which tabs. Its functions <code>join</code>, <code>leave</code>, <code>update</code> (new metadata) and
				<code>sweep</code> return the changes others should hear (<code>Joined</code>, <code>Left</code> or
				<code>Changed</code>) and give the room's state a new number with each one. <code>Room</code> never reads
				a clock: the caller passes the time in, so its tests run on a clock they are given and test a timeout
				without waiting for one:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.presence_grace.file}, in part"} source={Sources.presence_grace.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Leaving has a grace period.</strong> A tab whose stream ends leaves after five seconds, unless
					it is back by then, so a page that reloads or reconnects doesn't flicker out and in. A page that drops
					the room while the tab stays open (it went to another room) leaves at once: its
					<code>on_close!</code> hook is told <code>Dropped</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Vanishing has a timeout.</strong> A page says it is here every 15 seconds. A tab unheard for 75
					seconds is swept out, however long its connection lingers. Browsers may slow a hidden tab's timers to
					once a minute, so 75 seconds is that minute plus one beat to spare.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Changes are numbered.</strong> A page's copy of the room (<code>Mirror</code>) takes a
					snapshot, then each change in order. A change older than its snapshot is ignored. A change that skips
					a number means one was missed, and the page asks for a fresh snapshot.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Kept in memory, swapped in.</strong> The server keeps each room in its
					<a href={Paths.platform_kv}>Kv</a> store. Each change reads the room, sweeps it, changes it, and
					writes it back only if no one else changed it in the meantime (<code>Kv.swap!</code>); otherwise it
					reads it again. So two tabs that join at once both count, and nothing is written to the database.
					What changed is then published on the room's channel, numbered. A room nobody speaks in for a quarter
					of an hour is forgotten.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Typing and cursors are passed on, never kept.</strong> The server forwards them to the other
					tabs. For a cursor only its latest place counts, so a tab that falls behind gets the newest place and skips
					the ones in between.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="stores">The stores</Heading>
			<p>
				The browser's side is a set of stores in the Solid style: functions you call in a component, as you
				call <code>create_signal!</code>, that keep their state in signals. <code>join!</code> is a whole room
				on its channel. The others are its parts, and take what a room says however it arrives, so you can feed
				them from something else, as the demo does.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Store</th><th>Gives</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>{"Presence.join!(channel)"}</code></td><td>the room on a route's channel, joined for as long as the component is on the page: everything <code>create_room!</code> gives, fed by the channel, and <code>others!</code> (everyone but you), plus what this tab says: <code>status!(status)</code>, <code>update!(data)</code>, <code>typed!()</code> and <code>done!()</code>, <code>moved!(point)</code> and <code>away!()</code>, and <code>visible!(shown)</code>. It tells the room this tab is here every 15 seconds, and says its <code>data</code> again then if the room has other data for it</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Presence.create_room!(on_gap!)"}</code></td><td>all the parts at once, fed a room's events with <code>heard!(event)</code>: <code>members!</code>, <code>synced!</code>, <code>me!</code> (this tab's key and id), <code>typing!</code> (others' keys), <code>cursors!</code> (others'), and <code>lost!</code> for when the stream drops. Someone who leaves stops typing, and their cursor goes</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Presence.create_presence!(on_gap!)"}</code></td><td>who is here: <code>members!</code>, <code>synced!</code>, <code>receive!(message)</code>, <code>lost!</code>. <code>on_gap!</code> runs when a change was missed</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Presence.create_typing!(ttl)"}</code></td><td>who is typing: <code>who!</code>, <code>started!(key)</code>, <code>stopped!(key)</code>. A ping lasts <code>ttl</code> milliseconds</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Presence.create_typist!(every, send!)"}</code></td><td>your own typing: call <code>typed!()</code> on each key and <code>done!()</code> when the text is sent or left. <code>send!</code> is given <code>Started</code> (at once, then at most every <code>every</code> milliseconds) or <code>Stopped</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Presence.create_cursors!({ idle, forget })"}</code></td><td>others' cursors: <code>shown!</code>, <code>moved!(move)</code>, <code>gone!(key)</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Presence.create_pointer!(every, send!)"}</code></td><td>your own pointer: <code>moved!(point)</code> and <code>away!()</code>. <code>send!</code> is given <code>Moved(point)</code> at once, then at most every <code>every</code> milliseconds (the latest place held and sent when due, so a cursor ends where the pointer stopped), or <code>Away</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Call each store at most once per handler. As in Solid 2.0, a handler's writes apply when the handler
				is done, so a second call in the same handler sees the store as it was before the first, and the first
				call's change is lost. A channel gives each event a handler of its own, so a page fed by one is safe. A
				store whose entries expire (typing, idle cursors) runs its own timer, every quarter second, only while
				something can expire.
			</p>
			<p>
				The demo above has no channel, so it does what one would: a press tells the stores what happened, one
				call each, and each cursor's move is sent from a timeout of its own.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.presence_stores.file}, in part"} source={Sources.presence_stores.rocx} />

			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<p>
				Under the components and the stores is plain Roc, on no platform, so the server's hooks and the
				browser's stores run the same code. The browser's half sees these modules through the package's
				<code>browser.roc</code>; the server's half also sees <code>PresenceServer</code>.
				<code>roc test packages/presence/main.roc</code> runs their <code>expect</code>s. The
				<a href="https://github.com/Roc-Solid/rocx/tree/master/packages/presence">package's README</a> lists
				every function.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Room</code></td><td>a room, as the server keeps it: <code>join</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>heartbeat</code>, <code>leave</code>, <code>quit</code>, <code>sweep</code>, <code>signal</code>; <code>members</code>, <code>version</code>, and <code>deadline</code> (when the next sweep could find someone); <code>save</code> and <code>load</code> for JSON; <code>Room.defaults</code> and <code>beat_every</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Mirror</code></td><td>a page's copy of a room: <code>receive</code> a <code>Snapshot</code> or a numbered <code>Delta</code> (<code>Err(Gap)</code> when one was missed), <code>members</code>, <code>is_synced</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Diff</code></td><td><code>between(before, after)</code>: who left, joined and changed; <code>apply</code> one change</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Throttle</code></td><td>at most one send every <code>every</code> milliseconds: the first at once, the latest held until it is due. A cursor sent 20 times a second ends where the pointer stopped</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Cursors</code></td><td><code>place</code> (a pointer's pixels as fractions of the area), <code>moved</code>, <code>gone</code>, <code>shown</code>, <code>sweep</code>, <code>coalesce</code> (a burst, one move per member), <code>percent</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Typing</code></td><td>who is typing: <code>started</code>, <code>stopped</code>, <code>who</code>, <code>deadline</code>, <code>sentence</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Typist</code></td><td>your own typing: <code>typed(now)</code> says when to ping, <code>done</code> when to say it stopped</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Stack</code></td><td><code>split</code> (who fits, and the <code>+N</code>), <code>initials</code> (<code>"Ada Lovelace"</code> is <code>"AL"</code>), <code>color</code> (the same key always gets the same colour), <code>names</code>, <code>label</code>; a face's <code>Status</code> and its <code>status_word</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Wire</code></td><td>what a room's channel carries: to a page, an <code>Event</code> (<code>Welcome</code>, <code>Presence</code>, <code>Typing</code>, <code>Moved</code>, <code>Away</code>); from one, a <code>Said</code> (<code>Here</code> with its status, <code>Update</code> with its data, <code>Typing</code>, <code>Moved</code>, <code>Away</code>); who is in it, a <code>Member</code> (a key and a <code>Meta</code>: name, image, color, status, data); <code>meta(name, data)</code>; and <code>face</code>, <code>names</code> and <code>pointers</code> to feed the components</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>PresenceServer</code></td><td>the server's side: a room channel's hooks, <code>on_open!</code>, <code>on_close!</code> and <code>receive!</code>, with the room kept in <code>Kv</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="styling">Styling</Heading>
			<p>
				The components take their sizes and colours from CSS custom properties. For the avatar stack:
				<code>--avatar-size</code> (2rem), <code>--avatar-overlap</code>, <code>--avatar-ring</code> (the ring
				between faces; set it to the page's background), <code>--avatar-font</code>, <code>--avatar-more</code>,
				<code>--avatar-away</code>, <code>--avatar-busy</code> and <code>--avatar-offline</code> (an offline face's
				opacity, 0.5). For the typing
				line: <code>--typing-color</code> and <code>--typing-size</code>. For cursors:
				<code>--cursor-glide</code> (0.12s; the demo's is 1.4s) and <code>--cursor-font</code>. Under
				<code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>, the dots stop bobbing and the cursors jump instead of gliding.
			</p>

			<Heading id="status">Status</Heading>
			<p>Works today:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>the modules, with their tests (<code>roc test packages/presence/main.roc</code>);</li>
				<li>the three components and the stores, which <code>rocx build</code> copies into an app;</li>
				<li>
					the server's side on a route's channel (<code>presence.PresenceServer</code>). In examples/board, two
					people in their own browsers each see the other, see which card each has open, see one go away while
					their tab is hidden, and see a third who closes their browser leave;
				</li>
				<li>your own cursor, sent as you move (<code>on_move!</code>), and a hidden tab shown as away (<code>here.visible!</code>);</li>
				<li>the app's own metadata, typed: <code>Wire.Meta(d)</code>'s <code>data</code> goes through the room and its channel as it is, and the page changes it (<code>here.update!</code>).</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Not yet: the live stream itself as the heartbeat (today the page sends one every 15 seconds), and a room
				named by the page's path without a route of its own (for a "who's viewing this page" stack). The
				package's PROPOSALS.md has the details.
			</p>
			<details>
				<summary>The demo: <code>{Sources.presence_demo.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.presence_demo.file} source={Sources.presence_demo.rocx} />
			</details>
			<details>
				<summary>The avatar stack: <code>{Sources.presence_avatar_stack.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.presence_avatar_stack.file} source={Sources.presence_avatar_stack.rocx} />
			</details>
			<details>
				<summary>The typing indicator: <code>{Sources.presence_typing_indicator.file}</code></summary>
